{
  "_doc": "Shared redacted-question fixtures. Both sides read this one file: Kotlin from\nRedactedQuestionWireTest (off the jvmTest classpath) and JavaScript from\nfunctions/question-redaction-wire.test.js (by relative path). It is the only thing holding\nRedactedQuestionContent.kt and functions/question-redaction.js to the same wire shape.\n\nEvery case in \"redacted\" is a round trip through the shipped emitter. JavaScript runs\nredact(JSON.stringify(source), documentDifficulty, {random: seeded(seed), questionId}) and\nasserts the result equals 'publicPayload' character for character; Kotlin decodes\n'publicPayload' into the RedactedQuestionContent variant named by 'expectedType' and asserts it\nsurvives a round trip back out. So the emitter cannot change shape without this file changing,\nand this file cannot change without Kotlin noticing. 'seed' drives the mulberry32 generator in\nfunctions/_seeded-random.js, which both JS suites require rather than each keeping a copy.\n\n'reEncodesExactly' says which round trip is claimed. True means byte identity: Kotlin's encoder\nreproduces 'publicPayload' exactly. False means only a semantic round trip — decode, encode,\ndecode again, and the two decoded values are equal — because the emitter omits a key whose\nKotlin field has a default, and encoding writes it back out explicitly. Three such keys exist,\nand all three are shapes the real corpus produces: a payload with no 'protectedTextSegments',\none whose source carried no 'id' (every seeded question — the id lives on the wrapper document),\nand one with no 'difficulty' at all. Both kinds of case still have to decode and still have to\ncarry no answer.\n\n'difficulty' is a string here, never an enum. resolveDifficulty copies a payload's own value\nverbatim on purpose — parseQuestionPayload merges {...fallback, ...parsed}, so a payload's empty\nstring already wins downstream and substituting anything would change what the question is worth\n— so '' and 'MEDIUM' are both shapes this emitter is written to produce, and both are pinned\nbelow beside the ordinary EASY/HARD ones.\n\n'forbiddenKeys' is the list of key names no public half may carry, read by both suites so that\nneither owns it. They are matched as *keys*, walked recursively — never as substrings of the\npayload text, since 'order' and 'info' are ordinary words that appear in question prose.\n\n'parseMustSucceed' is the regression half: the ordinary and legacy payloads whose handling this\nslice must leave exactly as it was. Kotlin runs them through QuestionContentParser and asserts\nsuccess; JavaScript asserts that none of them wears a redacted discriminator, and that the\nSurvey case comes back 'not-applicable' — 'there is no redacted Survey' is a load-bearing claim\nin the Kotlin type's own KDoc.",
  "forbiddenKeys": [
    "correctOptionId",
    "correctOptionIds",
    "correctCandidateId",
    "blankToCandidate",
    "order",
    "idMap",
    "info"
  ],
